WEBVTT

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:03.570
CHRIS ANDERSON: Hi.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:04.770
Thanks for watching the video.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:05.730
My name is Chris Anderson.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.740
I'm an Engineer on
the Windows UI team,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.070
working on some new experimental
frameworks features

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:11.610
going forward.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.110
And I want to talk
about some of the stuff

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.840
that we've been working
on behind the scenes

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.660
and really trying to move this
to more of in the public.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.030
So welcome.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.580
First, I really want to spend
the time, before I dive

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.530
into the new and exciting things
that I think are attractive

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.500
and shiny bobbles in the
feature, I want to talk

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.510
about the stuff that
we need to get going

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.940
so that we can earn the right
to build some of these things.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.400
And this is really
the core features

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.760
that people have been
asking for and really need

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.470
to be productive
on top of WinUI.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.590
The first and foremost is
performance, fundamentals,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.810
quality, fixing a lot of bugs.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.510
On the performance side,
we've invested heavily

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.650
in really improving memory
usage as well as switching

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.700
over to a system compositor,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.700
which should yield some even
better performance improvements.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.450
And this stuff all actually hit
the public in our Git repo.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.090
And so it should be available
to go play with today.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.430
And there will be
WinApp SDK versions

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.980
that incorporate these
changes available shortly

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.410
through the experimental
preview branches.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.720
We're also adding in a
lot of new controls.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.060
We have DataGrid and Charting
that are up and coming

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.800
that should be out
relatively shortly.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.870
These will be showing up
in the core WinUI bits

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.390
and will allow you to
go after a lot more

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.610
of these data-oriented
scenarios.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.410
One of the big things that
I'm personally really excited

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.260
about is for us to complete
our journey to open source.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.360
We've started on this
path quite a while ago.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.280
We recently hit our Phase 3,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.590
which was when we could
actually run tests in public.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.650
And so anyone can grab
the bits, compile WinUI,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.210
and then run the test to make
sure that they have something

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.230
that is fully functional.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.880
If they want to make changes,
they can run the test

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:45.900
and make sure they
haven't broken anything,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.690
which is an important part of
having an open source library.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.660
The big next phase
for us is Phase 4.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.880
And really, that is
where we move the team

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.500
from using internal source
repos to working primarily

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.590
and almost exclusively
in the public repos.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.390
And so you'll see the
team start to land all

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.300
of their pull requests in
the public repo side of it.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.040
And that's really just
about getting to a place

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.860
where we can accept changes from
the community, we can engage

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.390
in the community, and the
community can see the work

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.000
instantly as soon as
we've got it going.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.990
With these changes, we've really
heard a common set of themes.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.050
Probably the number
one thing is,

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.640
are you guys actually
serious this time?

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.200
Are you going to stick
with this framework?

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.930
WinUI 3 is four years old.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.430
Are you going to keep
going, or is this the year

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.770
that you're going to announce a
brand-new framework yet again?

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.750
And I will say that, no,
we have no intention

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.310
of building a new framework.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.590
In fact, we're dropping the
number, and we're referring

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.650
to WinUI as just WinUI
because we have no intention

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.490
of really making a massive
shift, breaking change on it.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.460
And we're really trying to
push hard to stretch WinUI

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.220
to be accessible for any
use case that we want.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.530
We've started to integrate
it into the shell

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.940
at a much faster rate.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.390
And so you're going to see a lot

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.860
of the first-party
features coming

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.200
from Microsoft being
built on top of WinUI.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.220
And we hope to see a lot

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.790
of third-party developers
really start to adopt it.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.420
We've seen some pickup already,
but we know that the move

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.510
to adopt a new UI framework
can take a long time,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.860
especially in enterprise spaces.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.580
And so we see this as our
time to really start showing

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.080
that we are putting
our muscle behind this

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.390
and that we're committed
to move WinUI forward.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.150
And that is fully our intention.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.670
The second thing we've heard
a lot is, beyond DataGrid

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:30.150
and Charting, there's just a ton
of feature gaps in the platform.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.130
Everything from what are
you doing on system tray?

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.410
And you have limited
use of Windows.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.480
A bunch of people use
open source libraries

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.840
to fix these gaps, and can we
just get the platform fixed?

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.460
And so we have a large backlog.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:42.540
We're going to start
working through that

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.030
and really start trying to
address the feature gaps

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.410
that people are calling out.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.690
But it's been one of
the biggest issues.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.360
The third thing that
is a constant source

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.360
that people bring up is, how
are we going to migrate?

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.980
How are we going to go
from the current set

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.480
of frameworks we have?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.340
Can we just incrementally
adopt WinUI?

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.170
Or are you making an
all-or-nothing proposition

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.450
where you have to
rewrite everything?

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.380
We have a reasonable story
of WinForms interop today.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.970
It's not great, but it's
a reasonable story.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.500
It works pretty well.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.310
But we want to build on that.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.380
We want to make it that that
should be a bulletproof part

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.590
of the story, that WinForms
interopting with WinUI

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:20.010
in either direction should
be just super easy to do.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:20.880
So we intend to do that.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.400
We want to make WPF
migration equally good

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.560
that you should be able
to mix and match WPF

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.810
and WinUI with no problems.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.090
And so that's a big chunk
of work that we want

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.830
to get to in the future.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:37.110
What we've also seen is that the
development style is changing

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.340
quite a bit.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.960
The AI assistance has changed
the way people write software.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:43.980
It's changed the way
I write software.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.260
I rarely type
semicolons anymore.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.360
I'm almost always
using AI to drive most

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.980
of the code that I'm writing.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.000
I use it for code reviews,
for writing tests.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.410
I have it help me with specs.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.920
It's like this whole notion
of this AI shift has really,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.010
really transformed the way
you write all the code.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.590
At the same time, we've
seen this journey of more

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:08.040
and more dynamic UI continue.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.080
It accelerated in a lot of ways.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.500
And I look at things
like SwiftUI, Compose,

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.710
and React as examples
of the best practice

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.890
in the industry today for
building these dynamic UI.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.050
And it becomes super
straightforward to build things

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.450
that are incredibly
flexible and responsive

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:25.350
to what the user does.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.020
And so that's another
trend that we really see.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.200
And the second one is really
this notion of getting to code

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.480
as the primary way
to write things.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.610
On one way, you can
say that this move

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.350
to CLI-based coding tools with
Claude Code, GitHub Copilot,

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:45.840
things like this have led to
Visual Studio Code becoming one

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.690
of the most productive places
to be to work on your project

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.430
with your terminal
window next to you.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:51.660
And this really gets to a world

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.930
where code first becomes
the de facto standard.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.660
And so what we really
want to do is get C#

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:01.830
as a primary way
to target WinUI.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.820
We want to elevate C# from being
a way to do code behind for XAML

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.640
to a way that you can actually
write an entire application.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.930
So you should be able to build
an existing WinUI framework,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.720
use the controls, the templates.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.340
All of those features
should be accessible

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.380
from a C#-first experience.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.720
Several of those are currently
locked behind needing

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:22.170
to have XAML.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.050
And so we really want to
bring C# up to parity.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.440
We're also announcing today a
new experimental framework,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.800
which is called Reactor.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.120
Microsoft UI Reactor is a
way for us to experiment

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.900
with the new styles of
programming, new controls,

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.200
and new app models as
we think about it.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.740
The Reactor repository, you
should think of as a place

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.270
for us to experiment with ideas

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.090
that we don't necessarily
have full conviction behind,

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.920
and that we really want to
engage with the community

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.660
and do development
out in the open.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.760
And so it was started
as an open project.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.050
It is very early.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.260
It is a high churn rate.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.950
We're changing lots of the
code on a daily basis.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.970
So you should really think of
this as a place that is open

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.210
for a ton of feedback, a ton of
guidance from the community,

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.920
a ton of participation
from the community.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.590
We already have PRs landing
from some of the community,

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:18.390
and we would like to get more.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.220
And the idea is that
as we find good ideas

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.900
and as we bake those ideas,
we'll be pushing them

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.680
down into the production WinUI
bits, and that they will,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.590
at that point, become
consistent and standard

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.260
across the WinUI stack so
that it doesn't stand out.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.950
So you may see things that look
foreign in the Reactor layer.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.560
And really don't panic
about that stuff.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.230
It is a place for us to try
these ideas out and then see

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.780
which of these makes sense
to go get pushed in.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.100
So really, the main
action I would say

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.210
for people watching this video
today is please pick up WinUI.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.110
Go build something with it.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.220
Keep using it.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.240
Keep making it forward.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.630
We have no plans
on deviating off

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.700
that as our primary
way of moving forward.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:57.660
We're investing in it.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.130
We hope that you will too.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.350
And then the last thing is,
let's go try out Reactor.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.520
Give us feedback.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.560
Go clone the repo
and try it out.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.360
Let's start by diving in
and showing you what's

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:10.000
in the new Reactor repo,
and let me show you that.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.630
Let's dive right into
Microsoft UI Reactor,

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.200
which is a new open source
project that we're releasing

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.290
that shows the future direction

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.210
that we're thinking
of for WinUI.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.090
It includes a bunch of
controls that are experiences

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.710
that we think are going
to be delivered as part

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.800
of the core WinUI bits using
the XAML you know and love,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.080
data binding, the
MVVM architecture,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.880
all the features
you're looking for.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.440
As well, there's included

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.770
in the project a new
domain-specific language

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.910
or a new projection
into C# for all

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:46.310
of the WinUI controls
that are out there.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:48.440
And this allows you to use C#

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.110
with your primary programming
experience if you're the person

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:52.940
who would like to do
a more functional,

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.060
reactive-style
approach as opposed

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.920
to an XAML-based approach.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:02.460
The library itself, if you go
look at the open source project,

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:03.930
you'll find that
almost everything

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.930
in there is written
using this new C# style.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.820
That's mostly because
we wanted to experiment

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.100
with this new style
of programming

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.740
as we built these controls
to see what it felt like

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.680
and where the edges were
of the thing of it.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.650
We don't really expect that this
is going to be the right way

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.720
to build most of these controls.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.480
A lot of them are going to be
integrated directly into WinUI.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.940
They'll be written in C++ using
the same WinRT frameworks

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.560
that you're familiar with.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.650
This is just more of a way for
us to experiment and learn.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.860
And so don't take that just
because you see DataGrid that's

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.670
in there, that's written using
this Reactor-style syntax,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:39.480
it doesn't mean that's
how we're going

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:40.710
to eventually ship the DataGrid.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.510
This is more of just an
experimental playground.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.370
This is a good time
to give this context

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.140
on what I mean by experimental.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.290
This is a place where we are
likely to change every line

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:50.340
of code in this project.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.390
We are very early.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.640
We want to do the
development out in the open,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.350
which means that we're going
to change a lot of things.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:56.400
We're going to
change the syntax.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.940
The current syntax is using
this fluent functional style

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.730
expression, but that
may completely change.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.500
We're working with the C#
team about the right way

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.160
to express these kinds of
construction patterns.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.720
And we really want
to do it in a way

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:11.430
that feels very natural
in the language.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:12.120
And so we're going to iterate.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.380
We're going to change
a lot of stuff.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.140
Now is really a chance for
you to play with the team

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.700
in the project and take a look.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.790
We've already had some
contributors from outside

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.400
of the company who've added some
new features to the product

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.340
and have given a lot of
feedback, have issued some PRs.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.950
So we're really looking
forward to collaborating

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.320
with the community
on all of this.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.740
So let's dive in and look at the
code and how this functions.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.050
So we see here we have
a "ReactorApp.Run,"

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.450
which is just starting up the
project, creates a window.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.030
The devtools flag, we'll
talk about it a little bit.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:43.380
It's something that
you'd normally only put

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:45.090
in a debug build
of your product.

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:46.710
And then here we
have a component,

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:48.720
which is the main
unit of composition.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:50.640
And we "override Element
Render," create a title bar,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.710
heading, text block, put
it in a flex column.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.800
And that's what you see
over here on the left.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:55.880
So the first thing
I'm going to say is

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.620
that the text block
is hard to see,

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.330
so let's just make
the font size larger.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.300
We'll save, and then we're
going to come over here,

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:08.700
and we'll create a flex
column because I want

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.250
to take this header
and Hello text

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.260
and indent them a little bit.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:19.290
So I'm going to just say
"Margin24," and then I also want

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.970
to have it occupy all the space.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:25.440
So I'm going to say
"grow:1, basis:0."

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.780
Save that, and we see
that the app is updating.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:28.830
Down here on the bottom left,

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:33.000
I'm just running a normal
standard.NET watch command,

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.980
and up on the left
is the app running.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.480
At some point, this might crash.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.880
It turns out hot reload,
not always the most stable.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.620
We will see how this goes.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.010
But in the meantime, we can
just continue to iterate.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.860
So we can see basically
hot reload is working.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:48.000
We can create our functions.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.050
We have a nesting.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.290
In this case, we can do
imperative construction.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:55.110
We can also do more of your
expression-based construction.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.410
Whatever you'd like to do,
it's your place to play.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.000
Let's make this
interactive, though.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.260
So let's create some state.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:06.630
So we'll say we want
a count, UseState.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:07.440
It could be 0.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.690
And then we'll just have a name.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.980
Feels like what you need to do.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.200
And we'll just say "name."

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.860
And then we'll go
update our text here.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.260
And we'll just use "name."

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:32.910
And then we'll say
"count: count."

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.170
Great. So we see it updates.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.820
We got the name of the text.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.560
And like you see,
as you'd expect,

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.680
you can do any C# function
you want in here.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.660
Let's make this interactive.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:46.540
So the first thing we'll do
is we'll add a "nameField."

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:51.900
And we'll just say it takes
a name and it sets a name.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.450
And then we'll have
a row of buttons.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.350
We'll just have a "FlexRow."

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:03.700
And we'll create a
button that's "-."

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:10.000
And for that, we'll just say
set count to "count -1."

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:15.270
And then we'll
have a button "+."

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.760
And we'll do
"setCount, count+1."

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.830
Great.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.600
If I can type today.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.130
And then last, I want
to just put some space.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:34.350
So I'll say "with
ColumnGap = 4."

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.750
Great. And let's add these
into our header here.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.660
So we'll have it
be the name field.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.660
And then we'll have the buttons.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.200
"Save." We've got
our name field.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.500
The buttons are a little weird,
but we'll just type the buttons.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.360
We can type in the
name field now.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.510
And if I click "+," we see
that the count updates.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.210
Perfect. We'll get that little
layout glitch there fixed

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.770
in just a second here.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.370
Let's continue.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.570
So we have basic
state, single state.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:04.830
Let's do a little richer one.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.750
Let's do collection.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.430
So we'll just say I
want "Enumerable"

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.630
that is "Range to 100."

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.450
And we'll say, let's
just take the index,

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.070
and we'll create another string.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:28.080
We'll just say "Item # i."

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.220
And we'll do a "Guid.NewGuid.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:35.700
ToString" and grab
eight characters of it.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:37.950
And we'll make that into a list.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:43.690
Great. If we want to visualize
this, let's create a "listView."

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.940
And we're going to take
the items we just created.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.920
We need to create
a "keySelector."

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:55.030
So we're just going to say,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.110
because all of our items are
unique strings, pretty easy.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.390
We can do that.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.340
I have a little typo here.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:06.840
Let's go fix that.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:09.960
And then we need to
create a "viewBuilder,"

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.130
which is just how we
want to create it.

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:12.540
So we'll say "item."

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:13.710
We don't need a second argument.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:16.830
And we'll create a "TextBlock"
with the item in it

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:21.480
and a margin of 8 all around it.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.640
And last, just
because I don't want

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.190
to have it animate too much,
we'll put that in here.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:30.780
And we'll add our list
item to our listView here.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.030
And because we're putting
it in a flex grid,

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:36.060
I want to have this guy
be the largest piece.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.820
So it's going to fix that
little glitch we saw earlier,

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.950
also because that will consume
the rest of the space.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.590
So now we see our list of items.

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:44.430
They're in a list box.

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:45.390
They scroll.

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:46.140
And we click "+."

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.280
Everything works.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:48.930
But the first thing you should
notice is that when I click "+,"

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:50.880
all of these become new guids.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.070
That's because this render
method is being called

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:53.880
every frame.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.290
Every time you make a
change, it's being called.

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.000
It's recomputing the display.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.980
And then we're diffing
between the two things.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.500
So we only update the things
that are actually changed.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.420
And in this case, we're
rerunning this every time.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.650
So these state variables,

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:07.320
because we did UseState,
they stick around.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:10.650
And so here we'll do a
"UseReducer," which is the name

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.530
for the thing that we do
that is a collection value.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:16.770
And now we'll go in
here and say we want

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:19.530
"items" and "updateItems".

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:21.060
And so this is how we'll
be able to mutate this.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:22.140
And if we save this,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.030
we'll notice right
away it fixes the bug.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:25.650
We can increment and decrement.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:26.520
We can change our text.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.250
And these are no longer
recomputing on every frame.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.070
Great. Quite an improvement.

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.690
Let's go and actually update it

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.190
so we can add something
to that list.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.780
So add "Add" button.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:44.580
And we'll say what we want
to do is "updateItems."

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.770
And this, you take in a
list, you return a list.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.080
And so it's going to be
the list that's coming in,

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.720
it's going to be called "list."

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.260
And it will say what we want
to return is a new list.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:59.820
And the first item we'll
have is "New item #,"

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:05.080
and we'll do "list.Count + 1."

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:08.490
And then I won't
bother with the guid.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.800
And then we'll just give
the rest of the list here.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:15.000
We've got our basic thing.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:15.720
We'll save that.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.730
And now, if we come over
here and click "Add,"

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:20.130
we see we can add the
new items at the top.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:22.180
Everything still functions.

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:27.360
One thing I'd like to show,
I want to demonstrate,

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:29.130
I've been talking about
this diffing thing

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.540
and this incremental update.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:31.620
And so let's go take
a look at that.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:34.560
So I mentioned before, we
had this DevTools idea.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:40.350
And so the idea is that we
hypothesize that there is a case

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.990
to have some more
developer-friendly things built

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:43.980
into the framework.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.750
And so here, what I'm going
to do is I'm going to turn in

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.840
and say, I want to have this
"DevToolsMenu" show up.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.990
Now, this DevToolsMenu actually
is automatically conditional

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.630
on you having set this
DevTools property

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:56.070
that you wanted
to opt into this.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:56.970
And then on the command line

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:57.990
that you actually
specified you'd

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:00.060
like to run this specific
instance with these on.

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.010
And this lets you turn
on developer features

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.440
in your product so you can debug
it better, you can use things,

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:06.120
and you can just write
conditional code

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:07.590
pretty trivially.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.780
We have a couple of
features built in.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.940
So here I'm going to say
highlight reconciler changers.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.610
And now, when I click "+,"
you'll see that it shows that,

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:15.840
oh, we're just
updating the string

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:16.800
because that's one big string.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.260
And if I click the text, you can
see we also are updating the

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.150
text box as we do it.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.490
And so, really,
this is just a way

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:26.070
to visualize what is
changing each frame.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.410
And so what I'd like to do
is demonstrate what happens

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:29.220
with a list like this.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:30.570
So let's go into our list.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:35.700
And instead of just doing a
TextBlock, let's do a "FlexRow."

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:39.480
And we'll put a second thing,
which is another "TextBlock"

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:44.130
that says ""Count: " + count."

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:46.540
And we'll do the
same margin of 8.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.220
Great. And so now we see,
we saw a big red thing

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.590
because it got
re-rendered completely,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.030
like new elements created.

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:55.020
But now, when we click "+,"

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.020
you'll see all we're doing
is changing that portion.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.790
But this top one, we're
changing the whole thing.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:03.150
And that's because this is one
text block that says "Hello"

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:04.110
and it does a big string.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:05.760
And so we have to update
that whole string.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:06.990
That's an atomic thing.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:09.630
But here we were able to
update only the Count part

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.730
because that was a separate
element, and we're able

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:14.440
to do incremental
updates to that element.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:19.180
Great. Let's turn off
the flashy blinky.

00:19:23.750 --> 00:19:27.620
Great. So the next thing
I'd like to touch on is how

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:28.910
to build new components.

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:30.320
So here we have this flex row.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.020
The easiest way to build a new
component is I can just simply

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.640
extract a method.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:40.260
And we'll call this
"ListItemView."

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:43.470
And it turns out that's
all we had to do.

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:44.340
This is the new function.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.540
This the new component.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:46.500
We don't have to
do anything else.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:47.820
It just works.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:50.070
So we're able to add, in fact,
if I change this and say,

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.700
actually, I want this
to say "Countish."

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:55.450
And we save it.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.830
Sometimes you get a crash.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:09.210
As promised, the.NET watch
is not always a thousand

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:10.470
percent reliable.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.510
Great. While we're waiting for
that to load, let's go through

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.040
and do the next thing that
we could create here.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:23.960
I'll just go make a quick change
here to force the reload.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:27.090
So instead of just
creating a function,

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:28.860
you can also create
a full component.

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:30.900
And so the component
gives you a life cycle.

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.660
It lets you know when
the component is mounted

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:34.530
into the tree.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.420
There's an update method
you can override.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:40.050
And so if I want to create a
new component from scratch,

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:41.760
it's a little more work
than just a function.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:44.640
And so what I'll do here is
I'll create "ListItemView."

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.980
I'll delete this version.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.640
And we see that we have a class
that derives from component.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:52.110
It declares what
properties it takes,

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:53.640
in this case, an
item and a count.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:57.540
It has roughly the same
content that was in there.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.160
And then we have a
little helper method

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:06.510
that gives you the
convenience function call

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.130
of just calling it
ListItemView instead of having

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:09.630
to do a bunch of construction.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.080
And so what I'll do is I'll
come up here and I'll add

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:16.080
"using static Components."

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.050
And so now this list item
view is now binding to that.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:25.510
And so if I save, it's still
working and no real change.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.360
We think.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:40.140
All right.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.340
We'll try to get
that going again.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:47.730
Great. Beyond the list
view, I want to show one

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:48.510
of these new controls.

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:50.430
We talked about new controls
that we're thinking of adding

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:51.510
to the platform
that we're looking

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:53.190
at that we want feedback on.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:55.080
And so let's go
replace this list view

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:56.490
with something a little richer.

00:21:56.490 --> 00:22:01.060
And so what I'll do is I'm going
to go and replace this with a --

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:12.810
We'll replace this
with a "PieChart."

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.090
And PieChart takes
in the list of data

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:17.640
that you want, in
this case, items.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:22.260
And then you have to provide it
with a way to get a function

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:24.460
for the individual component.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.780
So I need to get the
value, so we'll say "d,"

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:31.740
and we're just
going to return 1,

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:35.200
so everything is a unit 1 thing.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:41.850
And then I will just
say I'd like to have --

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:44.080
See if this refreshes.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:55.150
Come on.

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:04.680
I have to have code that
compiles for it to work.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.340
There we go.

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:09.360
So probably a few, two things.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.950
Let's just take the first five.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.160
That's better.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:16.110
But what I'd like to do is
have a label on these things.

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.450
And so we'll say the
LabelView that I'd

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:22.930
like to use is taking a item.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:26.680
Too much noise in there.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:33.750
And we'll just return
"ListItemView" of that item

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.970
and the count property.

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:39.210
And so now we see our counts,
and in fact if we "+,"

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:42.570
we see that they
increment as they should.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:44.430
Those are a little hard to
see, so let's go in here

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.700
and just say, we can
do a little offset.

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:52.890
So we'll say "LabelRadiusOffset"
and make it be 30.

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.090
And we say, okay, great,
they're out there.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:55.290
But, actually, let's do this.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:59.430
Let's make this be "15 * count."

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:04.020
And now, as we plus and minus
the count, those labels move.

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:06.930
So you can see this system
is really giving this a lot

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:08.910
of flexibility of
what you can do,

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:12.630
how you can transform the code,
and that the UI just continues

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.010
to update and keep up with it.

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:17.310
But so far, everything we've
shown is synchronous code,

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:18.570
which works really well.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:20.280
But what I'd like to do
is see what this looks

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.980
like if we get
asynchronous code.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:26.070
And so here what I
have is a resource,

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:28.020
which is an asynchronous value.

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:29.550
And I've just hard-coded it.

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.440
At the moment, it's just
a delay for two seconds.

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:34.650
And then randomly, every 20%,
let's just have it pretend

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:37.440
that it fails, just because
that's what the network does.

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:40.320
This guy had a little different
code in what it wants to show,

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:43.230
so we'll leave that
so we can see it.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.480
And we see if we compile,
it doesn't work right now,

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.370
so we got to, first off,
we can't update this item

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:50.580
because it's now an
asynchronous thing coming in.

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:53.880
And then last, you'll see that
this isn't even a list yet.

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.550
It says there is
no take on this.

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.440
And so what we need to do
is get the data out of

00:24:58.440 --> 00:24:59.520
that asynchronous result.

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:01.920
So we'll say "items.Match."

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:03.690
We're going to
return the element.

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:06.180
And we can basically set
what element we want

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:06.900
for different states.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:08.460
So for loading, we're going

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:14.970
to have it return a text
block that says "loading."

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:18.540
And if we get into an error
state, we'll ignore the error

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:24.430
for now and say, show me a
"TextBlock" that says "error."

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:37.920
And we'll make that be red.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:40.050
And then the last one we
can do is what happens

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:41.760
if we actually get
the data we wanted.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.950
Hey, that would be great.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:45.510
And so we'll say that
that takes something

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.130
which we'll call "data."

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:50.550
And now we can use our
function down here.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.480
And we'll say "data."

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:57.720
And now, if we save, we see
it says "loading" briefly.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:25:59.160
Goes into an error state.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.460
So what I want to do is have
it that I can kick this

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:05.130
and have it try in
because we lost the race.

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:07.440
So what we'll do is we'll say,
hey, whenever name changes,

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.380
pretend that's a dependency
of this resource.

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:12.910
And so now I can come in here,

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:17.190
and if I change the name,
it goes to "loading."

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:18.600
And we won the
lottery this time,

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:20.040
and so we get to see the value.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:21.480
So you can see that
what we've built

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:26.370
in is easy-to-use asynchronous
functions that can allow you

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:29.520
to handle the normal
cases you see in UI,

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:32.580
be able to very easily show
loading states, error states.

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.980
And you can do this with
any asynchronous values.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:37.810
You can have lists that
return these, etc.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:44.370
So we've now seen the breadth of
the main core pieces of what's

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:46.290
in Microsoft UI Reactor.

00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:47.790
It's an experimental framework.

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:50.610
We have a new set of controls,
which, like Charting,

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:53.130
would be something that's
just generally in WinUI.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.130
It's not going to be specific
to the Reactor syntax.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:59.340
Very few things would ever be
specific to the Reactor syntax.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:00.960
But we can play
with this new way

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.810
of doing C#-first development
and see how it feels,

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:04.590
see what it looks like.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:08.130
Please go try out the repo,
clone it, build something

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:12.810
with it, send feedback, file
feedback items, issue a PR.

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:14.280
Let's go see what we can
do with this and see

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.050
if this is something
that is interesting

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:18.870
to the community,
and learn together.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.200
Thank you.
